<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Corbinian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 670–<a href="/years/730">730</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Saint Corbinian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Corbinian was a Frankish missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> who worked at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freising">Freising</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> around 720 and is regarded as the founder of its diocese. According to legend, a bear killed his pack horse on the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and was made to carry his baggage, and the 'Corbinian bear' appears in the arms of Freising and of Pope Benedict XVI.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/corbinian.jpg" alt="Corbinian" width="320" height="365" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Polack · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Corbinian_polack.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Corbinian was born and baptised as Waldegiso at Châtres, near Melun, in Frankish territory, and was named after his father, who may have died when he was an infant. His mother, Corbiniana, then renamed him after herself. Nothing else is known of his childhood. The early source for his life is the Vita Corbiniani written by Bishop Arbeo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freising">Freising</a>. For fourteen years he lived as a hermit at Châtres, on the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, near a church dedicated to Saint Germain, until his reputation drew students who distracted him from his solitude.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Devotion to Saint Peter the Apostle led him to travel to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> with some of his disciples. There <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-ii">Pope Gregory II</a> urged him to evangelise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. Corbinian, who may already have been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> or was consecrated by Gregory, was sent to Grimoald, the Frankish Duke of Bavaria. On a mountain near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freising">Freising</a>, where a sanctuary already stood, he built a Benedictine monastery and a school, dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-vitus">Saint Vitus</a> and later to Saint Stephen, which became Weihenstephan Abbey in the 11th century.</p><p>Corbinian denounced Grimoald's marriage to Biltrudis, the widow of the duke's brother. She called him a foreign interloper and arranged to have him murdered, and he fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freising">Freising</a> until Grimoald was killed and Biltrudis carried off by invaders in 725. He returned at the invitation of Grimoald's successor, Huebert, and worked at Freising until his death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Corbinian was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merano">Merano</a>. In 769 Bishop Arbeo translated his body to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freising">Freising</a>, and it now lies in Freising Cathedral. His feast day is 8 September, and the translation of his relics is commemorated on 20 November. His brother Erembert governed the monastery after him and in 738, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a> created four dioceses in the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, became the first Bishop of Freising. The bear of his legend has been read as the bishop himself tamed by God's grace, the pack saddle as the burden of the episcopate. Joseph Ratzinger adopted it on becoming Archbishop of Freising-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> in March 1977 and kept it as pope.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Corbinian” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Christianisation was carried out by missionaries such as Rupert at Salzburg, Emmeram at Regensburg and <b>Corbinian</b> at Freising around 700, and in 739 Boniface organised the bishoprics of Salzburg, Regensburg, Freising and Passau.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The diocese goes back to <b>Saint Corbinian</b>, who worked at Freising around 720, and was formally organised by Saint Boniface in 739.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-freising">Prince-Bishopric of Freising</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freising">Freising</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.45,1.49&amp;year=709" aria-label="Chartres on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4585 470 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Corbinian"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5035 740h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5035 740h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.45,1.49&amp;year=709">What was here in 709 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
